+---
+name: tone-of-voice
+description: Winston Ferguson's tone of voice for the consulting site and client-facing copy. Use when writing or editing any page copy, outreach message, case study or proposal.
+---
+
+# Tone of Voice Guidelines
+
+Nov 10, 2026 · Winston Ferguson
+
+*An operator who moved into software, talking plainly to someone who runs a workshop.*
+
+## The voice in one line
+
+Speak like an experienced operator talking shop to a peer: plain, specific and honest, never like a vendor or a consultant selling a method.
+
+The reader is the owner or ops lead of a UK made-to-order business. They are busy, not technical, and wary of AI hype and of anyone selling them software. They decide in seconds whether a page is about them, so the voice has to sound like someone who has run a business, not someone pitching to one.
+
+## Five principles
+
+| Principle | Do | Don't |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Start with their problem | "Quotes take hours and get re-typed into production." | "I help SMEs unlock digital transformation." |
+| Be specific | Name the step, the tool, the number: "a 5-page report and a ranked list of fixes." | "A comprehensive, tailored solution." |
+| Sound like an operator | Use shop-floor words: quote, workshop, cut list, job, spec. | Use consultant words: stakeholders, workflows, synergies. |
+| Stay calm and honest | Say when software isn't the answer, and say what you don't know. | Promise transformation or hype AI. |
+| Write to one person | "I", "you", short sentences, direct questions. | "We", "our team", long abstract sentences. |
+
+## Words to use and avoid
+
+Check the buyer words below with 3–5 owners before locking them in. The "niche word" is still open.
+
+| Use | Avoid | Why |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| quote, quoting | quotation workflow, CPQ | Owners say "quote". |
+| the workshop, the shop floor | production environment | Their place, their word. |
+| job, order, spec | project, deliverable | Matches how they talk. |
+| re-typing, re-keying | data silos, manual processes | Concrete, visible pain. |
+| fix, tool | solution, platform | Plain nouns. |
+| made-to-order (test with owners) | bespoke, mass customisation, digital transformation | Check which word they use. |
+| AI, only where it earns its place | revolutionary, cutting-edge, game-changing | Sceptical buyers tune out hype. |
+| audit, teardown | strategy, roadmap, engagement | Says what happens next. |
+
+## Before and after
+
+The left column is from the old site and from your draft About paragraph. The right column is the same idea in this voice.
+
+| Before | After |
+| --- | --- |
+| I'm a London-based multidisciplinary professional specializing in technology, design, and management. | I help made-to-order businesses turn quotes into production without the rework. |
+| The landscape is moving rapidly and it's a unique time to create and deploy specialized tools cost effectively in SMEs. | Custom software is now cheap enough for small makers to afford. |
+| My background makes me uniquely positioned to run an audit and propose solutions that work for you. | I've run businesses and I build software, so I can tell you what's worth fixing, with or without code. |
+| AI can be the direct solution or indirect solution by making software development more accessible. | Sometimes AI is part of the tool. More often it makes building the tool faster and cheaper. |
+
+## How the voice flexes
+
+The voice stays the same; the dial moves with the setting.
+
+| Where | Dial | Rule of thumb |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Homepage | Warmest, shortest | Their problem first, one button. No history of you above the fold. |
+| Case studies | Most factual | Problem, what I built, result. Numbers where you have them. No adjectives. |
+| About paragraph | Personal, still plain | The operator-who-moved-into-software story in 3–4 sentences. |
+| Outreach email or message | Casual, direct | Two to four lines, one question about how they quote today. No pitch. |
+| Proposal | Clearest, most structured | Scope, deliverables, price, next step. Plain terms, no jargon. |
+| Posts or articles | Practical | Teardown style: here is a quoting flow, here is where the hours go. |
+
+## Open choices and defaults
+
+These are the points still open from our discussion. I've picked a default for each so copy can start; change any of them and the guide updates.
+
+| Choice | Default | Confirm by |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Lead identity | Operator who moved into software, not engineer who understands business | Your call; your latest draft already leans this way. |
+| Formality | First-name, friendly and direct | Your call. |
+| Niche word | "made-to-order" | Ask 3–5 owners which word they use. |
+| Name for the offer | "Quote-to-production audit" in proposals; buyer words in headlines | Test with owners. |
+| Datafurn mention | One honest line on About and Contact, kept out of the services pitch | Your call. |
+| AI in headlines | Not in the headline; mention it only where it earns its place | Your call. |
+| Price on the site | Not shown | Revisit after the first audits. |
